// A `return`/tail whose value the compiler auto-wrapped into an `Optional` or
// a `Result` is still a transfer, and is still judged.
//
// DF-305a (SL-79), closed by design 269 (SL-211). The checkpoint used to run
// AFTER the auto-wrap, so it judged the synthesized
// `OptionalWrap`/`ResultOkWrap` node instead of the author's expression; a wrap
// node was not an aliasing expression, so the transfer passed silently. This is
// DF-299a's shape at a third site: a checkpoint that RUNS and cannot see
// through the node, not one that is never called (DF-304a, closed).
//
// The fix PEELS a wrap at the checkpoint — one place, so every entry point gets
// it — rather than reordering the three sites that wrap. The full position
// matrix, the four wrap kinds and the ruling this carries (the OWNED source
// `func f(r: Res) -> Res? { r }` is refused too, one rule for both spellings)
// are conformance row V88.
//
// At an OWNED source the wrap really does transfer — codegen clears the
// source's drop flag — so the hole is invisible there. At an ALIASING source,
// below, it is a DOUBLE FREE at exit 0: the borrow capture hands out storage
// the enclosing frame still owns and releases, and `deinit` runs on `81` twice.
// The named-function twin (`func f(h: Holder) -> Res? { h.inner }`) is the same
// mechanism through `_check_function`'s tail and double frees identically.
//
// DF-304a judges the closure TAIL before the wraps, which is why the tail
// spelling of this program is already refused; the `return` spelling routes
// through `_check_return_statement`, which wraps first.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: without `move` in closure

struct Res { w: Int }
extension Res: NoCopy {
    func deinit(&var self) { print("drop {}", self.w) }
}

func run_opt(body: () sync -> Res?) -> Int {
    let got = body()
    if let g = move got { g.w } else { 0 }
}

func main() {
    var o = Res(w: 81)
    print("n {}", run_opt({ [&var o] in return o }))
    print("end")
}
